// app:ui — the mouse: which node owns a screen cell, and where a mouse event
// goes.
//
// ── Coordinates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **A mouse event's `x`/`y` are framebuffer pixels on every platform, the
// same unit the grid's cell size is measured in, so a cell is `x / cell_w`
// with no DPI factor.** Established from sokol_app.h itself, not assumed:
//
//   - the `sapp_event` field comment and the "MOUSE LOCK" section of its
//     header documentation say `mouse_x`/`mouse_y` are "absolute mouse
//     positions in framebuffer pixels";
//   - each backend scales the platform's logical position up before storing
//     it: macOS `_sapp_macos_mouse_update_from_nspoint` multiplies by
//     `dpi_scale` (and flips y to top-down), Emscripten multiplies `targetX`
//     by `dpi_scale`, Win32 `_sapp_win32_mouse_update` multiplies by
//     `dpi.mouse_scale` (1 when the framebuffer is full resolution,
//     `1 / window_scale` when `high_dpi` is off and the framebuffer is
//     logical), and X11 `_sapp_x11_mouse_update` passes the window pixel
//     through, the X11 window being sized in device pixels already;
//   - `app:input/sokol`'s decode hands `e.mouse_x`/`e.mouse_y` to
//     `input.push_mouse` unchanged, so `Event.x`/`Event.y` are those numbers;
//   - `grid.cell_w`/`cell_h` are in framebuffer pixels (the grid comes up at
//     the display's DPI scale), and `frame.dado` sizes the screen from
//     `display.window_get_size`, which is `sapp_width`/`sapp_height` — the
//     framebuffer size, not the logical window size.
//
// Before the grid is up the cell size reads 0, and a cell is then taken as one
// pixel, so a test with no window places the pointer exactly by passing cell
// coordinates as `x`/`y`.
//
// ── The hit map ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Which node owns a cell is an array read per layer, not a tree walk: one
// owner handle per screen cell **per layer group**, written by the paint walk
// into the map of the layer the node paints into (`set_layer`). `hit` reads
// the maps from the topmost layer down and answers the first live owner that
// is in view on its own layer, so a floating subtree owns the cells it covers
// and, once hidden, removed or moved, the node under it answers again without
// any repaint below. A map above the base is made at its layer's first claim.
// The base map answers as it always has: a stale owner there is `NIL`.
//
// Within one layer the rule is the one there has always been. Every node the
// walk repaints —
// whether or not its kind has a paint hook, because a container's area is
// where its gaps are — records itself as the owner of every cell of its
// clipped rectangle, before its children do, so the deepest painter owns a
// cell. The map is therefore exactly as current as the picture: whatever
// rule makes a cell's pixels right after a partial repaint (a node repaints
// its own cells; a parent repaints the cells a removed, hidden or shrunken
// child left) makes its owner right too, and an idle frame, which paints
// nothing, writes nothing.
//
// Owners are stored as full handles, so an owner removed since the last paint
// fails the generation check and reads as `NIL` rather than as whatever node
// has reused its slot.
//
// ── Routing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Keys and text go to the focused node; a mouse event goes to the node under
// the pointer — or, while one holds it, to the node with the capture — and
// bubbles to ancestors the same way. A press that some node handles captures
// the mouse for that node until every button is up again, so a drag that
// leaves the node's rectangle keeps reporting to it.
//
// ── The cursor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The system cursor is set when the cursor the frame wants changes, and at
// no other time** — not once per motion event, and not once per frame. What
// the frame wants is the *effective* cursor at the pointer's cell: the node
// holding the capture while one does, else the node under the pointer
// (`hovered`), asks its kind's `cursor` hook, and each ancestor in turn is
// asked while the answer is `CURSOR_INHERIT`; nobody answering is
// `CURSOR_DEFAULT`. So a TextEdit's I-beam survives a drag that leaves it,
// because the capture keeps asking the TextEdit, and a relayout that slides a
// different node under a still pointer changes the cursor without a motion.
// The frame asks (`sync_cursor`) after its layout and paint, on every frame —
// the answer also moves with no event and no paint, as when a program's
// `process` calls `capture` or `release` — and hands a changed answer to the
// sink: `display.set_cursor`, or whatever `set_cursor_sink` installed. Asking
// is cheap: a capture check or one hit-map read per layer, then a climb
// through the target's ancestors calling their `cursor` hooks. Before the
// first mouse event nothing is asked.
package ui

import "app:display"
import "app:input"
import "app:ui/cell"
import "app:ui/grid"

// One map per layer group, each one owner per cell of `g_screen`, row-major;
// the base's is made by the first `update` that sees a screen with cells, the
// others at their first claim. Every live map is `g_owner_n` long.
private [grid.MAX_LAYERS]ref []Control g_owners
private i32              g_owner_n
// How many claims the paint walks have written, ever. Read by the tests to
// see an unforced repaint skip its claim; nothing else reads it.
private i32           g_owner_writes

// The capturing node's slot, -1 for none. `g_capture_auto` is whether a press
// took it (and a buttonless event ends it) rather than `capture`, which lasts
// until `release`, the node's removal or hiding, or the window losing focus.
// `g_capture_touched` counts every
// `capture`/`release`, so a press handler that did either is not overruled by
// the automatic capture after it returns.
private i32  g_capture = -1
private bool g_capture_auto
private i32  g_capture_touched

// The last cell a mouse event named; `hovered` reads the map there.
private bool           g_have_pointer
private cell.CellPoint g_pointer

// ── Coordinates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `v / size` rounded toward negative infinity, in cells. A size below one is
// taken as one. `v` is clamped first so that no float the platform could
// report — an absurd one or a NaN, which lands on the negative bound —
// reaches the integer conversion out of range.
private i32 cell_of(f32 v, i32 size):
    f64 s = 1.0
    if size > 1:
        s = f64(size)
    f64 q = f64(v) / s
    if !(q >= -1000000000.0):
        q = -1000000000.0
    if q > 1000000000.0:
        q = 1000000000.0
    i64 t = i64(q)
    if f64(t) > q:
        t = t - 1
    return i32(t)

// The screen cell under a mouse event's position. See the package comment
// for the unit; negative positions give negative cells, which `hit` answers
// `NIL` for. Any event is accepted — a non-mouse one names whatever its
// `x`/`y` hold, which is `(0, 0)` for a key.
cell.CellPoint event_cell(input.Event e):
    cell.CellPoint p = (cell_of(e.x, grid.cell_w()), cell_of(e.y, grid.cell_h()))
    return p

// ── The hit map ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Size the map to `screen` and forget every owner. Called by `update` when it
// sees a new screen, which is also when it makes every node paint-dirty, so
// the paint walk that follows refills every cell the root covers.
private void size_owners(cell.CellRect screen):
    i64 n = 0
    if screen.cols > 0 && screen.rows > 0:
        n = i64(screen.cols) * i64(screen.rows)
    if n > 2147483647:
        n = 0
    // Every map above the base is given back; its layer's next claim remakes
    // it at the new size.
    for l in 1..<grid.MAX_LAYERS:
        if g_owners[l] != nil:
            #delete(g_owners[l])
            g_owners[l] = nil
    if n > 0 && (g_owners[0] == nil || #len(g_owners[0]) < n):
        if g_owners[0] == nil:
            g_owners[0] = #make([]Control, n, g_alloc)
        else if !#resize(g_owners[0], n):
            #delete(g_owners[0])
            g_owners[0] = nil
        if g_owners[0] == nil:
            // No map: `hit` answers `NIL` everywhere rather than reading one
            // that does not match the screen.
            g_owner_n = 0
            return
    g_owner_n = i32(n)
    for k in 0..<g_owner_n:
        g_owners[0][k] = NIL

// Layer `l`'s map, made (all `NIL`) at its first use; nil when it cannot be.
private ref []Control owner_map(i32 l):
    if l < 0 || l >= grid.MAX_LAYERS || g_owner_n <= 0:
        return nil
    if g_owners[l] == nil:
        g_owners[l] = #make([]Control, i64(g_owner_n), g_alloc)
        if g_owners[l] == nil:
            return nil
        for k in 0..<g_owner_n:
            g_owners[l][k] = NIL
    return g_owners[l]

// Forget every owner of `r` in layer `l`'s map: the cells a floating subtree
// left, so the layers under it answer there.
private void disown(i32 l, cell.CellRect r):
    if l < 0 || l >= grid.MAX_LAYERS || g_owners[l] == nil || g_owner_n <= 0:
        return
    cell.CellRect on = cell.intersect(r, g_screen)
    if cell.is_empty(on):
        return
    for row in on.row..<on.row + on.rows:
        i32 base = (i32(row) - g_screen.row) * g_screen.cols - g_screen.col
        for col in on.col..<on.col + on.cols:
            g_owners[l][base + i32(col)] = NIL

// Give back every map; `reset` calls it.
private void free_owners():
    for l in 0..<grid.MAX_LAYERS:
        if g_owners[l] != nil:
            #delete(g_owners[l])
            g_owners[l] = nil
    g_owner_n = 0

// Record slot `i` as the owner of every cell of `clip`. Called by the paint
// walk for each node it repaints, before its children.
//
// Skipped when the paint was not forced, the node has no children and it
// already claimed exactly this clip, because then nothing can have written
// over its claim: an ancestor that repainted would have forced this paint, a
// new screen clears every claim, and a hide or removal clears the claims of
// the node and its parent. A widget that repaints one damaged line on each
// keystroke therefore writes no ownership at all. A node with children always
// writes, since a child that shrank or moved gives cells back to it with its
// clip unchanged. What the skip gives up: where siblings overlap, a sibling
// that repaints alone does not take cells from the later sibling that owns
// them — the map keeps what a full repaint makes, the later sibling on top.
private void own_cells(i32 i, cell.CellRect clip, bool forced):
    if g_owner_n <= 0:
        return
    if !forced && g_nodes[i].first_child < 0 && g_nodes[i].claimed == clip:
        return
    g_nodes[i].claimed = clip
    g_owner_writes = g_owner_writes + 1
    ref []Control m = owner_map(g_nodes[i].layer)
    cell.CellRect r = cell.intersect(clip, g_screen)
    if m == nil || cell.is_empty(r):
        return
    Control c = handle(i)
    for row in r.row..<r.row + r.rows:
        i32 base = (i32(row) - g_screen.row) * g_screen.cols - g_screen.col
        for col in r.col..<r.col + r.cols:
            m[base + i32(col)] = c

// The node that owns screen cell `(col, row)`: in the topmost layer that has
// a live owner there, in view and still on that layer, the deepest node whose
// last paint covered it. `NIL` off the screen, before the first `update`,
// where nothing has painted, and where the base's owner has been removed or
// hidden (or an ancestor of it hidden) since — until the repaint that gives
// the cell to the parent. One array read and one flag per layer map made.
Control hit(i32 col, i32 row):
    if g_owner_n <= 0 || !g_have_screen:
        return NIL
    if !cell.contains(g_screen, col, row):
        return NIL
    i32 k = (row - g_screen.row) * g_screen.cols + (col - g_screen.col)
    i32 l = grid.MAX_LAYERS - 1
    for l > 0:
        if g_owners[l] != nil:
            Control u = g_owners[l][k]
            i32 j = index(u)
            if j >= 0 && g_nodes[j].in_view && g_nodes[j].layer == l:
                return u
        l = l - 1
    if g_owners[0] == nil:
        return NIL
    Control c = g_owners[0][k]
    i32 i = index(c)
    if i < 0 || !g_nodes[i].in_view:
        return NIL
    return c


// The node under the last mouse event's position, read from the map now, so
// it follows a relayout without the pointer moving. `NIL` before any mouse
// event. No enter/leave events are sent.
Control hovered():
    if !g_have_pointer:
        return NIL
    return hit(g_pointer.col, g_pointer.row)

// ── Capture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Send every mouse event to `c`, wherever the pointer is, until `release`,
// until `c` is removed or hidden, or until the window loses focus; a press,
// a release and a buttonless motion do not end it. The focus loss does,
// because it is the one moment the program is told the pointer may have gone
// somewhere it will not hear from — a popup holding the mouse closes then
// anyway, and a capture that outlived it would take the first click after
// the window came back. `NIL`, a dead handle and a node out of view release.
// Replaces any capture held, automatic or not.
void capture(Control c):
    g_capture_touched = g_capture_touched + 1
    g_capture = index(c)
    if g_capture >= 0 && !g_nodes[g_capture].in_view:
        g_capture = -1
    g_capture_auto = false

void release():
    g_capture_touched = g_capture_touched + 1
    g_capture = -1
    g_capture_auto = false

// `NIL` for a node that is not live or not in view; hiding one ends its
// capture anyway (`set_visible`), so this is belt and braces.
Control captured():
    if g_capture < 0 || g_nodes == nil || !g_nodes[g_capture].live || !g_nodes[g_capture].in_view:
        return NIL
    return handle(g_capture)

// ── Dispatch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private const u32 BUTTONS = 0x700

// Offer `e` to `from`'s input hook and then each ancestor's until one answers
// true; answers whether one did and which. A hook may remove nodes: the climb
// goes on to the parent read before the call only while that parent is still
// live, and a handler that removed itself still counts as handling the event
// but is answered as `NIL`, so nothing is handed a dead node.
// The climb ends after slot `stop` has been offered it, when `stop` is not -1.
private (bool handled, Control by) bubble(Control from, input.Event e, i32 stop):
    Control at = from
    for valid(at):
        i32 i = index(at)
        Control up = handle(g_nodes[i].parent)
        InputFn fn = g_kinds[i32(g_nodes[i].kind)].hooks.input
        if fn != nil:
            if fn(at, g_nodes[i].state, e):
                if valid(at):
                    return (true, at)
                return (true, NIL)
        if i == stop:
            break
        at = up
    return (false, NIL)

// Offer `e` to `c`'s own input hook and to nobody else — no bubbling, no
// focus or capture change — and answer whether it handled it. For a widget
// that drives another from its own keys, as a line edit drives a list's
// selection while keeping focus (`line_edit_set_key_target`). False for a
// dead handle, a kind with no input hook, and while `update` runs.
bool offer_input(Control c, input.Event e):
    i32 i = index(c)
    if i < 0 || g_phase != PHASE_IDLE:
        return false
    InputFn fn = g_kinds[i32(g_nodes[i].kind)].hooks.input
    if fn == nil:
        return false
    return fn(c, g_nodes[i].state, e)

// The mouse half of `dispatch`; answers whether a node handled `e`.
//
// A press focuses the nearest focusable node from the target up, before the
// event is offered, so the handler already sees itself focused; a press over
// nothing focusable leaves focus where it was — clicking empty space is not a
// request to drop the keyboard.
//
// A release or a motion that arrives with no button held ends an automatic
// capture, after the capturing node has seen it. Any such event, not only the
// release, because a release can be lost — the button let go outside the
// window, or while another window had the pointer — and a capture waiting for
// it would send the next press, anywhere on the screen, to the node that
// captured last. A wheel notch does not count: its `mods` carry no button
// bits even mid-drag (see `input.MOUSE_WHEEL`), so it says nothing about the
// buttons. A press always carries its own button's bit.
//
// While a modal is open (modal.dado), a mouse event whose target is not inside
// it is swallowed — answered as handled and offered to nobody — and a press
// among them emits `outside_pressed` on the modal, which is how a dialog
// learns it was clicked away from.
private bool dispatch_mouse(input.Event e):
    input.MouseKind k = input.mouse_kind(e)
    g_pointer = event_cell(e)
    g_have_pointer = true
    Control target = captured()
    if target == NIL:
        target = hit(g_pointer.col, g_pointer.row)
    i32 m = modal_index()
    if m >= 0 && !(valid(target) && in_subtree(index(target), m)):
        return modal_outside(m, k)
    if k == input.MouseKind.Press:
        Control f = target
        for valid(f) && !is_focusable(f):
            f = parent(f)
        if valid(f):
            set_focus(f)
    i32 touched = g_capture_touched
    (bool handled, Control by) = bubble(target, e, -1)
    if k == input.MouseKind.Press && by != NIL && g_capture < 0 && touched == g_capture_touched:
        g_capture = index(by)
        g_capture_auto = true
    bool buttonless = k == input.MouseKind.Release || k == input.MouseKind.Motion
    if buttonless && g_capture_auto && (e.mods & BUTTONS) == 0:
        g_capture = -1
        g_capture_auto = false
    return handled

// The window lost focus: whatever the buttons do next, this program may not
// hear it, so every capture ends — explicit ones too (see `capture`).
private void focus_lost():
    g_capture = -1
    g_capture_auto = false

// ── The cursor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A hook's answer meaning "not mine: ask my parent".
const i32 CURSOR_INHERIT   = -1
// The cursors, re-exported from `app:display`, which maps them to sokol's.
const i32 CURSOR_DEFAULT   = display.CURSOR_DEFAULT
const i32 CURSOR_IBEAM     = display.CURSOR_IBEAM
const i32 CURSOR_POINTER   = display.CURSOR_POINTER
const i32 CURSOR_RESIZE_EW = display.CURSOR_RESIZE_EW
const i32 CURSOR_RESIZE_NS = display.CURSOR_RESIZE_NS

// Where a changed cursor is sent. Nil is `display.set_cursor`.
type CursorSink: void(i32)

private CursorSink g_cursor_sink
// What the sink was last told. sokol starts on its default cursor, so this
// does too, and a pointer that only ever crosses default-cursor nodes costs no
// call at all.
private i32 g_cursor_shown = CURSOR_DEFAULT
// Calls made to the sink, ever: what the tests count.
private i32 g_cursor_sets

// Send changed cursors to `sink` from now on; nil sends them to the display
// again. What the old sink was told is taken as shown.
void set_cursor_sink(CursorSink sink):
    g_cursor_sink = sink

// The cursor last handed to the sink, and how many times one has been.
i32 cursor_shown():
    return g_cursor_shown

i32 cursor_sets():
    return g_cursor_sets

// The effective cursor over `c` at cell `p`: the first answer other than
// `CURSOR_INHERIT` from `c`'s kind's `cursor` hook and then each ancestor's;
// `CURSOR_DEFAULT` when none answers, and for a dead handle. An answer below
// `CURSOR_INHERIT` is taken as `CURSOR_INHERIT`.
i32 cursor_at(Control c, cell.CellPoint p):
    Control at = c
    for valid(at):
        i32 i = index(at)
        CursorFn fn = g_kinds[i32(g_nodes[i].kind)].hooks.cursor
        if fn != nil:
            i32 want = fn(at, g_nodes[i].state, p)
            if want > CURSOR_INHERIT:
                return want
        at = handle(g_nodes[i].parent)
    return CURSOR_DEFAULT

// The cursor the pointer should show now: the capturing node's while one
// holds the capture, else the hovered node's, at the last mouse event's cell.
// `CURSOR_DEFAULT` before any mouse event.
i32 cursor_wanted():
    if !g_have_pointer:
        return CURSOR_DEFAULT
    Control target = captured()
    if target == NIL:
        target = hovered()
    // Nothing under a modal takes the mouse, so nothing there sets the cursor.
    i32 m = modal_index()
    if m >= 0 && !(valid(target) && in_subtree(index(target), m)):
        return CURSOR_DEFAULT
    return cursor_at(target, g_pointer)

// Hand the sink the wanted cursor if it is not the one shown. The frame
// calls this after `update`; a test calls it where the frame would. Does
// nothing before the first mouse event.
void sync_cursor():
    if !g_have_pointer:
        return
    i32 want = cursor_wanted()
    if want == g_cursor_shown:
        return
    g_cursor_shown = want
    g_cursor_sets = g_cursor_sets + 1
    if g_cursor_sink != nil:
        g_cursor_sink(want)
    else:
        display.set_cursor(want)
